---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Container from '../components/Container.astro';
import SectionIntro from '../components/SectionIntro.astro';
import TopicCluster from '../components/TopicCluster.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import PullQuote from '../components/PullQuote.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/FaqList.astro';
import FaqItem from '../components/FaqItem.astro';
import SiteCta from '../components/SiteCta.astro';
import EditorialPhoto from '../components/EditorialPhoto.astro';
import { withBase } from '../data/site';
import focusImage from '../assets/images/lala/lala-golden-hour-focus.jpg';
import emptyChair from '../assets/images/editorial/editorial-empty-chair.png';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Private Guidance | She Who Remembers"
  description="Individualized coaching for women in transition: self-trust, boundaries, and discernment, without handing your inner authority to a guide."
>
  <Hero
    heading="Private Guidance"
    lede="A place for the threshold that needs more room."
    surface="light"
    spacing="compact"
  >
    <p>
      Private work is individualized support for a specific season: a decision, a transition, a
      boundary, or an integration that will not fit neatly inside a group container.
    </p>
    <p>
      It does not replace inner authority. Its purpose is to make your own discernment more
      available.
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="Why private work">
        <p>
          Not every hard season needs a coach. Some need rest, friendship, clinical care, or
          simply time. Private guidance is for the threshold that wants a thinking partner and
          more room than a course can give.
        </p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <ul class="fit">
        <li>A relationship threshold that keeps repeating in the same conversation.</li>
        <li>Identity in motion: the self that still functions, and the one arriving underneath it.</li>
        <li>Boundary work that has become tangled, delayed, or expensive.</li>
        <li>Decision fatigue, and the habit of outsourcing the last inch of knowing.</li>
        <li>Self-trust that went quiet after too many second opinions.</li>
        <li>A creative or vocational turn that insight alone has not moved.</li>
        <li>Aging, changing roles, and a name that no longer fits the life.</li>
        <li>Family-pattern work that needs precision, not a courtroom.</li>
        <li>Purpose and legacy questions that are specific, not generic.</li>
        <li>Integration after a major chapter, when the event is over and the living is not.</li>
      </ul>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container>
      <SectionIntro heading="What we may explore">
        <p>The work follows what is actually in the room, not a syllabus you have to complete.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="topics">
        <TopicCluster title="Self and identity">
          Self-trust, identity change, sovereignty, decision-making, and the over-functioning that
          can impersonate a personality.
        </TopicCluster>
        <TopicCluster title="Boundaries and relationships">
          People-pleasing, resentment, communication, family roles, relational patterns, and
          cleaner limits.
        </TopicCluster>
        <TopicCluster title="Body and discernment">
          Embodiment, intuition, body signals, the difference between anxiety and knowing, and
          presence that can stay.
        </TopicCluster>
        <TopicCluster title="Voice and creation">
          Expression, creativity, purpose, visibility, and work or life transitions that ask for
          a truer sentence.
        </TopicCluster>
        <TopicCluster title="Integration and legacy">
          Aging, lived wisdom, contribution, lineage, and what comes next once healing is no
          longer the only subject.
        </TopicCluster>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="What a session feels like">
        <p>Serious, human, and uninterested in performance.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="photo-breath">
        <EditorialPhoto
          src={focusImage}
          variant="focus"
          alt="Lala in a cream wrap, eyes closed, hands resting together on a stone ledge."
        />
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The work is conversational. Reflective. Direct when a pattern is hiding in plain sight,
          spacious when rushing would only recreate the old speed. It is embodied and practical:
          what you will actually do with Tuesday, not only what you understand about yourself.
        </p>
        <p>
          You are not required to perform vulnerability, or to disclose on cue. Humor is allowed.
          So is silence. Attention stays with what is happening beneath the story you already
          know how to tell: the contradiction, the delayed no, the place language and behavior
          part ways.
        </p>
        <p>
          At its best the guide can function as mirror and midwife, sanctuary and forge. That is
          a description of stance, not a claim of magic. You remain the one living the life.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="The role of the guide">
        <p>Lala is not there to become your authority.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          She may reflect a pattern, ask a question you have been circling, notice a
          contradiction, slow the pace, challenge an assumption, help distinguish signal from
          story, and support integration into choices you can keep.
        </p>
        <p>
          She does not make life decisions for you. She does not tell you whether to stay or
          leave a relationship. She does not diagnose, prescribe treatment, or claim to know your
          intuition better than you do. Dependency is not the product.
        </p>
      </div>
      <PullQuote>
        Good guidance should make you more available to yourself, not more dependent on the guide.
      </PullQuote>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container>
      <SectionIntro heading="Ways to work privately">
        <p>
          Three containers. No prices or session lengths are listed here yet. A fit conversation
          is how we choose among them, or choose something else.
        </p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="offer-grid">
        <Card>
          <h3>Single session</h3>
          <p>
            For a specific question, decision, boundary, or threshold that needs a clear room
            and a thinking partner, not a long arc.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card>
          <h3>Six-session mentorship</h3>
          <p>
            For a focused stretch of change and integration: enough repetition to practice, not
            so much structure that the work becomes a lifestyle.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card>
          <h3>Twelve-session mentorship</h3>
          <p>
            For deeper work across identity, embodiment, relationships, voice, and legacy, when
            the season is larger than a single knot.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="What private guidance can support">
        <p>Grounded shifts, not a rewritten fate.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <ul class="fit">
        <li>Clearer self-contact, including when the answer is inconvenient.</li>
        <li>More accurate boundaries, with less theater around them.</li>
        <li>Better discernment between signal, fear, and old loyalty.</li>
        <li>Less over-explaining as a way to earn the right to exist.</li>
        <li>Stronger ownership of decisions that were previously put up for committee.</li>
        <li>More honest communication, including the sentences you have been postponing.</li>
        <li>Greater integration between what you know and how you actually behave.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">
        This work does not promise healed relationships, trauma resolution, mental health
        treatment, spiritual awakening, financial outcomes, or guaranteed transformation.
      </p>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="What this is not">
        <p>The edges keep the work honest, and they keep you safer.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <ul class="not">
        <li>Not psychotherapy, diagnosis, or psychiatric care.</li>
        <li>Not medical advice, legal advice, or crisis support.</li>
        <li>Not prophecy, and not a reading of your future.</li>
        <li>Not a command about whether a relationship should continue.</li>
        <li>Not a transfer of spiritual authority from you to the guide.</li>
      </ul>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="Fit matters">
        <p>Wanting support is not the same as this being the right room.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A fit conversation can help determine whether private guidance is appropriate, whether
          a group course would serve you better, or whether another kind of support is the more
          honest next step. There is no prize for forcing a match.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="photo-breath photo-breath--later">
        <EditorialPhoto
          src={emptyChair}
          variant="chair"
          alt="An empty wooden chair sits beside a sunlit window in a quiet plaster room."
        />
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="Questions">
        <p>What people usually want to know before they sit down.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <FaqList>
        <FaqItem question="How do I know if private guidance is right for me?">
          <p>
            If the threshold is specific and needs more room than a group container, private
            work may fit. If you want shared pacing and a named pathway, a course may be better.
            A fit conversation is where we sort that without requiring you to already know.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="Do I need to be in crisis?">
          <p>
            No. Crisis needs care built for crisis. Private guidance is for a season, decision,
            or integration that wants more room, not for emergency, danger, or acute mental
            health need.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="What if I am already in therapy?">
          <p>
            The two may coexist when their roles stay clear. Therapy addresses what belongs in
            clinical care. This is coaching and educational work: reflection, discernment,
            boundaries, and choice. It does not replace treatment.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="Will you tell me what to do?">
          <p>
            No. You may be asked harder questions than you arrived with. The decisions remain
            yours. A guide who needs to be followed is not doing this job.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="Can we work on relationships?">
          <p>
            Yes: your patterns, communication, limits, and the places you disappear. This is not
            couples therapy, and it will not instruct you to stay or leave. Those choices, if
            they come, belong to you.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="What if I am not spiritual?">
          <p>
            Then you are still welcome. Belief is not an entrance requirement. The work is
            spiritually open and practically grounded. Plain language is always available.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="What happens if this is not the right fit?">
          <p>
            We name it. Another doorway in this body of work, or an entirely different kind of
            support, may be more honest. Fit is a discernment, not a sales obstacle.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
      </FaqList>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <SiteCta
    heading="If the threshold needs more room"
    primary={{ label: 'Book a Fit Conversation', href: withBase('/book-a-conversation') }}
    secondary={{ label: 'Begin Here', href: withBase('/begin-here') }}
  >
    <p>
      Bring the actual question, even if it is still inelegant. We will see whether private
      guidance is the right container.
    </p>
  </SiteCta>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .photo-breath {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-start;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-lg);
  }

  .photo-breath--later {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .note {
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .fit,
  .not {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .fit li,
  .not li {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
    padding-left: 1.15rem;
  }

  .fit li:last-child,
  .not li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .fit li::before,
  .not li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.7em;
    width: 0.4rem;
    height: 0.4rem;
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border-accent);
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  .topics,
  .offer-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .offer-grid h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .topics,
    .offer-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: var(--space-xl);
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .offer-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
